From deb8333903e1df35853a130ce1b11a59d2d8eb6e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 04:40:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GLES] The chroma steps down by missed refreshes where
 the GPU has no timer

The Lanczos 3 chroma of the 4:2:0 pictures that Kodi draws itself
stepped down only by the GPU time of its pictures. A GPU without
GL_EXT_disjoint_timer_query never stepped down, and one that has it
with timestamps of no bits (GL_QUERY_COUNTER_BITS_EXT 0, which the
extension allows) was told to step down where its time said so, and
its time never came: kodi.log promised a step-down that nothing
measured.

The bits of the timestamps are now asked: a timer is usable only where
they are more than 0, in Kodi's renderer and in the window system's own
timers (native Dolby Vision, Dolby Vision rebuilt, HDR10 adapted), and
the log says which of the two is missing. A GPU without a usable timer
is measured by the refreshes that its pictures miss, the signal that
the step-down of Dolby Vision rebuilt already uses: the window system
reads the flip of each commit that carries such a picture from its
out-fence, and a flip more than 1.5 refresh periods after the one
before missed a refresh (CFlipChain). A window of 48 presentations is
behind when 5 % of them, 3 at least, missed their refresh, and two
windows behind in a row take the filter a level down; without the GPU
time the cause of a missed refresh is not known, so one window alone
never does. Where neither can be read (no out-fence), the log says so.

A GPU with a usable timer is measured by its GPU time alone, as
before: the same levels and the same bytes. Strong GPUs that miss no
refresh never step down.
---
